<%# The picture: a rail that stands where the words and the queue stand, and opens
    the same way. What it draws is Milkdrop — the thing that was in Winamp, the
    reason people left Winamp open with the screen off — rewritten for WebGL, and
    fed the twenty years of presets people wrote for it.

    data-turbo-permanent, like its two neighbours, and for one reason more than
    they have: behind this canvas is a live WebGL context and a graph tapped off
    the <audio>. Neither is a thing to build again because somebody clicked on a
    record. So the rail is carried through a visit whole, and the controller on it
    picks up where it left off.

    The player does not draw the picture. It owns the rail — it owns all three —
    and says only when it opens; Milkdrop and everything about it is the
    visualizer's own. What the player does put here is the one thing that was
    never Milkdrop's business: what is playing, over the top of it, on a picture
    that has the whole screen. %>
<aside id="visualizer-panel" data-turbo-permanent data-player-target="visualizerPanel"
       data-controller="visualizer panel"
       data-panel-name-value="visualizer" data-panel-edge-value="left"
       data-action="player:visualizer@window->visualizer#settle
                    player:playing@window->visualizer#follow
                    keydown@window->visualizer#hotkey
                    fullscreenchange@document->visualizer#refit"
       style="--panel: var(--visualizer)"
       class="panel rail shrink-0 flex-col overflow-hidden rounded-2xl surface">
  <%= render "shared/grip", side: "left-0" %>

  <%# Gone in full screen: a frame around a thing that is meant to have none. %>
  <div class="visualizer-chrome flex shrink-0 items-center justify-between gap-2 border-b border-white/10 py-3 pl-2 pr-3">
    <div class="flex min-w-0 items-center gap-1">
      <button type="button" data-action="player#toggleVisualizer" aria-label="Collapse visualizer"
              class="shrink-0 rounded-full p-1.5 text-neutral-400 transition hover:bg-white/10 hover:text-white">
        <svg class="size-4" viewBox="0 0 16 16" fill="currentColor" aria-hidden="true">
          <path d="M10.28 3.22a.75.75 0 0 0-1.06 0L4.94 7.47a.75.75 0 0 0 0 1.06l4.28 4.25a.75.75 0 1 0 1.06-1.06L6.56 8l3.72-3.72a.75.75 0 0 0 0-1.06Z"/>
        </svg>
      </button>
      <h2 class="truncate text-sm font-semibold text-white">Visualizer</h2>
    </div>

    <%# Where this was always going. A rail is a postage stamp; Milkdrop is a
        thing you put on and turn the lights off to. %>
    <button type="button" data-action="visualizer#fullscreen" data-visualizer-target="fullscreen"
            aria-label="Full screen" aria-pressed="false"
            class="shrink-0 rounded-full p-1.5 text-neutral-500 transition hover:bg-white/10 hover:text-white
                   aria-pressed:text-accent">
      <svg class="size-4" viewBox="0 0 16 16" fill="currentColor" aria-hidden="true">
        <path d="M2.75 2H6a.75.75 0 0 1 0 1.5H3.5V6a.75.75 0 0 1-1.5 0V2.75A.75.75 0 0 1 2.75 2Z"/>
        <path d="M13.25 2H10a.75.75 0 0 0 0 1.5h2.5V6a.75.75 0 0 0 1.5 0V2.75A.75.75 0 0 0 13.25 2Z"/>
        <path d="M2.75 14H6a.75.75 0 0 0 0-1.5H3.5V10a.75.75 0 0 0-1.5 0v3.25A.75.75 0 0 0 2.75 14Z"/>
        <path d="M13.25 14H10a.75.75 0 0 1 0-1.5h2.5V10a.75.75 0 0 1 1.5 0v3.25A.75.75 0 0 1 13.25 14Z"/>
      </svg>
    </button>
  </div>

  <%# Black, not glass. This is the one surface in the app that is not showing you
      the room behind it — it is the picture itself, and Milkdrop was drawn on
      black. %>
  <div class="relative min-h-0 flex-1 bg-black">
    <canvas data-visualizer-target="canvas" class="absolute inset-0 size-full"></canvas>

    <%# Milkdrop is not one picture, it is thousands, and each one is somebody's
        work with their name on it. So the rail says whose you are looking at. %>
    <p data-visualizer-target="preset" aria-live="off"
       class="pointer-events-none absolute inset-x-0 bottom-0 truncate bg-gradient-to-t from-black/80 to-transparent
              px-3 pb-2 pt-6 text-[11px] leading-tight text-white/70"></p>

    <%# What is playing, on a picture that has the whole screen — and only then.
        In its rail the pill sits under it saying all of this already; full screen
        the pill is behind the picture, on a page nobody can see, and somebody
        across the room asking what this is has to come out of full screen to find
        out.

        The player writes it, because what is playing is the one thing it has
        always known — it says the same three things in the pill and again on the
        lock screen of a phone, each at the size that screen wants them. This is
        the biggest of the three. %>
    <div class="visualizer-billing pointer-events-none absolute inset-x-0 bottom-0 items-end gap-6
                bg-gradient-to-t from-black/85 via-black/50 to-transparent px-10 pb-10 pt-24">
      <img data-player-target="screenCover" alt=""
           class="hidden size-44 shrink-0 rounded-xl object-cover shadow-2xl ring-1 ring-white/10">

      <div class="min-w-0 pb-2">
        <p data-player-target="screenTitle" class="truncate text-4xl font-semibold leading-tight text-white"></p>
        <p data-player-target="screenSubtitle" class="mt-1 truncate text-xl text-white/70"></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</aside>
